{
  "introduction": {
    "text": "The Biblioteca di Archeologia e Storia dell'Arte in Rome is Italy's largest library dedicated to art history and archaeology, serving scholars, students, and the public with a vast collection of rare books, manuscripts, and archival material.",
    "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"]
  },
  "topics": {
    "history": [
      {
        "id": "h1",
        "description": "Founded in 1918 as the Istituto nazionale di archeologia e storia dell'arte, with the aim of promoting studies in archaeology and art history.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1918,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h2",
        "description": "Inaugurated on 4 June 1922, the institute also incorporated the former library of the Direzione generale delle antichità e belle arti.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1922,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h3",
        "description": "In 1926, the library received the Louis Gonse collection of travel books and the Rodolfo Kanzler collection of theatrical posters.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1926,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h4",
        "description": "In 1929, the library acquired the Rodolfo Lanciani collection of Roman topography materials.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1929,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h5",
        "description": "After Corrado Ricci's death in 1934, his personal library of about 4,500 volumes was donated to the library.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1934,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h6",
        "description": "In 1939, due to the impending war, the library was temporarily moved to the Farnesina.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1939,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h7",
        "description": "In 1955, the library acquired the Ugo Monneret de Villard collection of Oriental archaeology and art history.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1955,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h8",
        "description": "In 1958, the library received the Giulio Quirino Giglioli collection of Roman and provincial art and archaeology.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1958,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h9",
        "description": "In 1967, a decree established the library as a state public library, increasing its acquisition budget.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1967,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "h10",
        "description": "In 1971, the Attilio Rossi collection of over 5,200 volumes was donated to the library.",
        "sourceIds": ["wikipedia"],
        "time": {
          "startYear": 1971,
          "precision": "exact",
          "granularity": "year"
        }
      },
      {
